## 5. Testing and Evidence Strategy

Four layers, each with a distinct job:

| Layer | What it proves | Count |
|---|---|---|
| Unit (Vitest) | Domain rules in isolation: allocation order, money arithmetic, numbering, reconciliation math, aging, export writers | 200 in 17 files |
| Integration (Vitest + real PostgreSQL) | Posting flows, transactions, triggers, permissions, concurrency against a disposable database | 186 in 11 files |
| E2E (Playwright + built Electron) | Desktop workflows through the real bridge: billing, payments, GCash, routes, receivables, backups, permissions | 21 |
| Manual checklist | Operator walkthroughs with expected/actual columns | `TEST_CHECKLIST.md` |

Supporting evidence:

- **Acceptance matrix** `docs/ACCEPTANCE.md` names the exact test title behind each of AT-01 to
  AT-12, so the matrix can be checked by searching the source rather than trusted.
- **Performance run** (`npm run test:performance`) builds 20,000 subscribers, 140,000 invoices,
  41,692 payments and 181,692 ledger entries and measures 19 real routes against budget, with
  required indexes named and no sequential scans.
- **Demonstration dataset** (`npm run db:seed:demo`) meets all twenty laboratory minimums in
  about 11 seconds, and `demo-dataset.test.ts` re-proves the accounting identities on top of it:
  billed = paid + open, paid = standing allocations, received = allocations + held credit,
  ledger net = open − credit.
- **Artifact round-tripping**: the PDF reader test takes the y coordinate of every row back out
  of the PDF content stream instead of trusting that the text still parses, and saved receipts
  are checked as bytes on disk.

---

## 6. Defects and Lessons Learned

Real defects found by the process, each with its root cause:

| # | Defect | Root cause | Lesson |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | The Phase 5 payment API suite (AT-01–AT-06) had been deleted in a later commit while the evidence documents describing it stayed in place | Documentation was written once and never regenerated from a run | Evidence must be produced *by running the suite*, and the acceptance matrix should quote test titles so they can be searched; tests are the evidence |
| 2 | Playwright discarded the worker after a failure and re-ran `beforeAll`, so the next test silently ran against a brand-new empty database (misread as an empty register and receipt numbers restarting) | Harness precondition was assumed, not asserted | Assert preconditions at the start of every E2E test; a failure must fail loudly, not reset the world underneath you |
| 3 | `coalesce(sum(...),0)::int` returned HTTP 500 once the receivable passed PHP 21,474,836.47 | 32-bit integer overflow in an office-wide money aggregate | Money aggregates need `bigint` end to end, and limits should be found by load tests, not by inspection |
| 4 | A password leaked through `err.stack` inside a log line even though message redaction existed | Redaction covered messages but not stack traces | Redaction must cover every field that can carry user data, including nested/serialized errors |
| 5 | Two PDF export defects (wrong page ordering, a mirrored row cursor) still produced files containing all the text and parsing as valid PDFs | Validity ≠ correctness | Read the artifact back structurally (coordinates, ordering) instead of trusting a parser's success |
| 6 | Remounting a tab with `key` to force a reload discarded the confirmation of the command that had just run | Using component remount as a data-refresh mechanism | Refresh by passing a revision/version value into the loading effect; never destroy UI state to reload it |
| 7 | The demonstration seed was refused 403 on an older database when setting a Phase 7 permission | `role_permissions` is written only by the security seed, never by a migration, so databases seeded before Phase 7 were never topped up | Seeds must be idempotent *and* additive — re-running one must repair anything a later phase added |
| 8 | Dependency resolution selected an unsupported Vite major and npm rejected the peer conflict; PowerShell execution policy blocked `npm.ps1` | Unconstrained installs and environment quirks | Pin supported majors and fix the environment (`npm.cmd`) rather than bypassing checks with `--force` |
| 9 | An early desktop flow exposed a `pg` DATE serialization defect and an accessible password-label issue | Driver response formats and a11y only surface in a real UI pass | Run the built desktop workflow, not just the API, before calling a phase verified |

Security and process lessons:

- **Never trust the renderer.** Every permission check is repeated on the server; AT-10 is proved
  by calling the API directly with a cashier's token, not only by hiding the button.
- **Never let a service be the only guard.** Database triggers and check constraints refuse
  edits, deletes and unbalanced postings even through raw SQL, which is what makes "immutable"
  a property of the data rather than of the code.
- **Two-person rules are structural**, not procedural: the GCash confirmer must not be the
  recorder, and the remittance signer must not be the counter — enforced by the API.
- **Keep phases visibly pending.** Nothing was checked off in `TASK.md` before its verification
  line was written from an actual run.

---

## 7. Security Posture

- Electron: context isolation, sandbox, no Node in the renderer, denied popups, navigation
  restrictions, CSP; session tokens live only in main-process memory.
- Authentication: scrypt password hashing, rate-limited sign-in, 8-hour sessions, lock/logout,
  session revocation on password/role/activation change, transactional protection of the last
  active Owner.
- Authorization: seven roles with permission rows; navigation is permission-aware but the API is
  the authority; direct-HTTP refusal tests exist for user, backup, report and payment operations.
- Data: proof uploads validate declared type against the actual bytes, generate names with
  exclusive-create, refuse paths outside the proof directory, record SHA-256, and remove the
  file if the transaction fails.
- Logging: one JSON object per line with request ids; bearer tokens, cookies, passwords,
  password hashes, proof bytes and connection strings are stripped from every field including
  error stacks; responses carry `nosniff`, `DENY` and `no-referrer`.

---

## 8. Known Limits (not claimed)

- Manual `PAY-*`, `COL-*`, `REC-*`, `SEC-01`–`SEC-03` checklist items have not been performed
  by a human; no receipt, route sheet or aging report has been sent to a physical printer.
- AT-09 is proved for concurrent API sessions against one PostgreSQL instance; a physical
  three-PC LAN test has not been done.
- The 24 screenshots have not been visually inspected by a human.
- `npm audit` reports 8 high-severity vulnerabilities in the `electron-builder` packaging chain
  (build-time only, no non-breaking fix) — recorded as AUTO-07 **Fail**.
- A single billing cycle is capped at PHP 9,999,999.99 by `moneyBounds` (≈10,000 subscribers
  per cycle at PHP 999/month); a FULL restore does not delete proofs added after the backup.

---

## 9. Final Metrics (verified 2026-10-07 for typecheck/lint/unit)

- `npm run check`: strict TypeScript, ESLint and **200/200 unit tests pass** on the current tree.
- Recorded on the same tree: 186 PostgreSQL integration tests in 11 files, 21 Electron tests,
  production build, `test:db` migration check and the 20,000-subscriber performance run.
- 35/35 `TASK.md` checklist items across Phases 1–10; AT-01–AT-12 all with automated evidence.
- 20 migrations, 31 tables, 66 API routes, 24 screenshots, 6 sample exports, 9 reconciled
  reports, 5 Mermaid ERD diagrams.

## Conclusion

The system meets the laboratory requirements with verifiable automated evidence, proper controls,
and comprehensive documentation. The most valuable outcome is not the feature count but the
discipline behind it: money is exact and immutable at the database level, permissions are proved
by calling the API directly, evidence is regenerated by running tests rather than written by
hand, and every defect found — a deleted test suite, an integer overflow, a password in a stack
trace, a PDF that parsed but printed wrong — is recorded with its root cause and a regression
test instead of being quietly fixed.
